tructure to store the result.\n113\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n114\t *\n115\t * @return 0 if successful, -1 if an error occurred.\n116\t */\n117\tint matrix2d_multiply(Matrix2D *m, Matrix2D *invm, Matrix2D *result,\n118\t                      uint64_t mod);\n119\t\n120\t/**\n121\t * @brief Perform a random swap of rows and corresponding columns between two\n122\t * matrices.\n123\t *\n124\t * This function randomly selects two rows in the matrix `m` and swaps their\n125\t * positions, simultaneously swapping the corresponding columns in the matrix\n126\t * `invm`.\n127\t *\n128\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n129\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n130\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n131\t *\n132\t * @return 0 if successful.\n133\t */\n134\tint swap_transform(Matrix2D *m, Matrix2D *invm, uint64_t mod);\n135\t\n136\t/**\n137\t * @brief Apply a linear mixing transformation between two matrices.\n138\t *\n139\t * This function applies a linear mixing transformation between two matrices `m`\n140\t * and `invm`. It randomly selects two columns in matrix `m`, and for each row,\n141\t * it adds a scaled value from the source column to the target column, where the\n142\t * scale factor is randomly chosen. The operation is performed modulo `mod`.\n143\t *\n144\t * Additionally, the corresponding rows in matrix `invm` are adjusted to\n145\t * maintain the inverse relationship with matrix `m`.\n146\t *\n147\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n148\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n149\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n150\t *\n151\t * @return 0 if successful.\n152\t */\n153\tint linear_mix_transform(Matrix2D *m, Matrix2D *invm, uint64_t mod);\n154\t\n155\t/**\n156\t * @brief Apply a scaling transformation to two matrices.\n157\t *\n158\t * This function applies a scaling transformation to the elements of two\n159\t * matrices `m` and `invm`. It randomly selects a scaling factor and its modular\n160\t * inverse, and then scales each element of both matrices by their respective\n161\t * factors. The operation is performed modulo `mod`.\n162\t *\n163\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n164\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n165\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n166\t *\n167\t * @return 1 if successful (typo correction: should be 0 if successful).\n168\t */\n169\tint scale_transform(Matrix2D *m, Matrix2D *invm, uint64_t mod);\n170\t\n171\t/**\n172\t * @brief Set the given matrix to the identity matrix.\n173\t *\n174\t * This function sets the elements of the given matrix `m` to form an identity\n175\t * matrix. An identity matrix is a square matrix in which all elements are zero\n176\t * except for the diagonal elements, which are set to one.\n177\t *\n178\t * @param m Pointer to the matrix to be set to the identity matrix.\n179\t *\n180\t * @return 0 if successful.\n181\t */\n182\tint matrix2d_eye(Matrix2D *m);\n183\t\n184\t/**\n185\t * @brief Fill randomly generated invertible matrix pairs using a set of simple\n186\t * linear transformations.\n187\t *\n188\t * This function fills randomly generated invertible matrix pairs `m` and `invm`\n189\t * using a set of simple linear transformations. For each iteration, it randomly\n190\t * selects a transformation from the available set of transformations and\n191\t * applies it to both matrices `m` and `invm`.\n192\t *\n193\t * The set of available transformations includes swapping rows and columns,\n194\t * scaling each element by a random factor, and applying a linear mixing\n195\t * transformation.\n196\t *\n197\t * @param m Pointer to the first matrix.\n198\t * @param invm Pointer to the second matrix.\n199\t * @param mod The modulus to apply to each element.\n200\t * @param iterations The number of iterations to perform, each representing an\n201\t * application of a transformation.\n202\t */\n203\tint fill_random_invertible_pairs(Matrix2D *m, Ma